<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <!-- D1: EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2 are off project-wide. The rule protects an object's invariants
       from callers that keep a reference to its mutable fields. Of the 219 findings on 2026-11-01:
       144 are JPA entity associations and collections, which must be the live references Hibernate
       manages (a defensive copy in a getter or setter detaches the collection from dirty checking and
       orphan removal, so edits are silently not saved); 27 are Spring beans holding constructor-injected
       singletons, which are shared by design; and the remaining 48 are records, DTOs and per-request
       holders (34 records, PeerEvaluationAverage, MyUserPrincipal wrapping the user entity, ...) built to be
       serialized or read and then discarded, which own no invariant over what they hold. None
       is a case of an object handing out state it needs to protect. SpotBugs filters cannot match
       by annotation, so narrowing to "non-entities" would be a hand-kept class list that rots. -->
  <Match><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/></Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
